// Reference query. Not deployed in any employer environment.
// Title: Application consent or permission grant that includes Tier 0, mail, or file permissions, and new credentials added to a service principal or application
// Status: untested template
// ATT&CK: T1528 (Steal Application Access Token), T1098.001 (Account Manipulation: Additional Cloud Credentials); ATT&CK v19.2
// Data sources: Microsoft Sentinel, AuditLogs (Microsoft Entra ID data connector)
// Licensing assumptions: Microsoft Sentinel with the Entra ID connector (scenario A-17); no add-on license needed for these audit events
// Rule ID: SN-03 | Platform: Microsoft Sentinel scheduled analytics rule | Suggested severity: High for Tier 0 or sensitive permissions, for any credential added to a Tier 0 workload identity, and for credential adds outside approved automation; Medium otherwise
// Tactics: Credential Access (TA0006), Persistence (TA0003)
// Answers: detection-priorities.md item 11 (Priority 2); attack-paths.md AP-6 steps 2 and 3
// Architecture context: IDS-ENTRA (app registrations and service principals), workload identities (03-identity-and-access.md section 6), Tier 0 workload identities (03 section 4) and residual risk RR-16, CA-22 coverage gap for managed identities and multitenant apps; risk register R-06
// Fictional scenario: Contoso Regional Health (fictional). Reference portfolio content.
//
// Purpose
//   An application token reads ePHI in mailboxes and files without a user session and without MFA. User
//   consent is restricted by design, so the remaining paths are a user who can still consent, abuse of
//   the admin consent workflow, or credentials added to an existing app. CA-22 does not cover managed
//   identities or multitenant apps, so this detection is the compensating control the threat model asks for
//   on the consent and credential side. It does not watch sign-ins:
//   managed identity sign-in monitoring is not built (coverage-map.md section 3, item 11).
//   An application permission is also a standing grant that no PIM activation or approval gates (RR-16),
//   so for a Tier 0 workload identity the alert on the grant, and on any credential added later, is the
//   control.
//
// Logic
//   1. Consent and grant activities from Microsoft's audit activity reference: "Consent to application",
//      "Add delegated permission grant", "Add app role assignment to service principal".
//   2. Credential activities: "Add service principal credentials" and "Update application - Certificates
//      and secrets management".
//   3. Consent rows are scored by a text match on the target resources. Permission names come from
//      Microsoft Graph permission tables. For an application permission, the name sits in the
//      AppRole.Value modified property of "Add app role assignment to service principal", where
//      Microsoft's Azure-Sentinel rule "Admin promotion after Role Management Application Permission
//      Grant" reads RoleManagement.ReadWrite.Directory; the text match covers that property.
//      - Tier 0 permissions (Tier0PermissionPattern): the application permissions in the table in
//        03-identity-and-access.md section 6, which makes an application granted any of them a Tier 0
//        workload identity (03 section 4): RoleManagement.ReadWrite.Directory,
//        AppRoleAssignment.ReadWrite.All, Application.ReadWrite.All, Directory.ReadWrite.All,
//        UserAuthMethod-TAP.ReadWrite.All, UserAuthenticationMethod.ReadWrite.All, the two read permissions
//        UserAuthMethod-TAP.Read.All and UserAuthenticationMethod.Read.All (Tier 0 until the lab test in 03
//        section 4), Policy.ReadWrite.ConditionalAccess, and Device.ReadWrite.All. Each is listed as an
//        application permission in Microsoft's Graph reference for the operation 03 section 6 names
//        (granting an app role assignment, adding a password to an application, creating a Temporary
//        Access Pass, creating a role assignment, updating a Conditional Access policy, updating a
//        device), and those references state a role requirement only for delegated calls.
//        The match is by name on every consent row, so it also returns delegated grants of these names.
//        A delegated grant reaches only what the signed-in user can reach (Microsoft's permissions
//        overview), so it does not make the application a Tier 0 workload identity; it still scores
//        High, with its own severity text.
//      - Sensitive permissions (SensitivePermissionPattern): permissions that reach ePHI in mail and files.
//   4. Credential adds by anyone other than the approved app-management automation score High. A
//      credential added to a Tier 0 workload identity (Tier0WorkloadObjectIdPattern) scores High whoever
//      adds it, approved automation included, because whoever holds the new credential can act as the
//      application.
//
// Known false positives
//   - Approved SaaS onboarding through the admin consent workflow (check the request ID and approver).
//   - Certificate rotation for single-tenant integration service principals, whose certificates are held
//     in a key vault and whose named owner is in the application owner registry (03 section 6); match the
//     change record. For a Tier 0 workload identity, every rotation alerts and is closed against its
//     change record.
//   - A Tier 0 permission granted to an application that must hold one, made by a Tier 0 administrator
//     against a change record (03 section 4). Close it against the record and add the application's object
//     IDs to Tier0WorkloadObjectIdPattern.
//   - Microsoft first-party service activity performed by service principals on the tenant's behalf.
//
// Tuning knobs (let statements below)
//   Tier0PermissionPattern        the permission names in the table in 03 section 6; an application
//                                 permission grant of any of them makes the application a Tier 0 workload
//                                 identity (03 section 4). 03 says
//                                 the table is not exhaustive, so keep this pattern in step with it.
//   SensitivePermissionPattern    other permission names treated as sensitive (mail and files).
//   Tier0WorkloadObjectIdPattern  object IDs of every Tier 0 workload identity, both the application
//                                 object and its service principal, from the owner registry (03 section
//                                 6). "Add service principal credentials" names the service principal and
//                                 the application update names the application. Never leave the pattern
//                                 empty: an empty alternation matches every row.
//   ApprovedAppAutomation         lower-case initiator names of sanctioned app-management automation.
//
// Response notes
//   Revoke the grant or remove the credential, disable the service principal if the consent is malicious,
//   review the app's sign-ins and mailbox and file access, and follow the identity compromise playbook.
//   Defender for Identity's Defender-format alert list also includes "Suspicious service principal
//   sign-in following credential addition", which complements this rule.
//   - Tier 0 grant, or a credential added to a Tier 0 workload identity, that no change record explains:
//     treat it as a suspected Tier 0 compromise (playbook decision point D4, Branch D), act from a Tier 0
//     PAW, and review what the application changed since, including grp-ca-emergency-access,
//     grp-admins-t0, grp-admins-t1, and the emergency access accounts (SN-07 alerts on those changes).
//   - Disabling a service principal that a clinical integration depends on can interrupt care: check
//     playbook decision point D5 first (clinical safety gate, ADR-008).
//
// Validation notes
//   Not run against any workspace. Validate in a lab tenant: register a test application, grant it a
//   delegated mail permission as a test user allowed to consent, and add a client secret to its service
//   principal. Expected telemetry: AuditLogs rows "Consent to application" and "Add service principal
//   credentials" (purple-team-plan.md exercise PX-12). Tier 0 grant branch: PX-20's optional application
//   step has an authorized lab administrator grant a lab application UserAuthMethod-TAP.ReadWrite.All as an
//   application permission and remove it afterward. As a further run in the same lab session, outside
//   PX-20, also grant and remove Policy.ReadWrite.ConditionalAccess, a 03 section 6 permission outside the
//   Temporary Access Pass row. Expected: each grant returns at High, with the permission name in
//   Tier0Permissions. Tier 0 credential branch: PX-12's second credential run adds a
//   credential to a lab application whose object IDs are in Tier0WorkloadObjectIdPattern, made by an
//   administrator after an approved activation of Application Administrator at tenant scope; repeat it
//   with the approved automation identity as the actor. Expected: "High: credential added to a Tier 0
//   workload identity" both times.
//
// References
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/monitoring-health/reference-audit-activities
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/permissions-consent-overview
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/user-list-messages?view=graph-rest-1.0
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/driveitem-get?view=graph-rest-1.0
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/serviceprincipal-post-approleassignedto?view=graph-rest-1.0
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/application-addpassword?view=graph-rest-1.0
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/authentication-post-temporaryaccesspassmethods?view=graph-rest-1.0
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/rbacapplication-post-roleassignments?view=graph-rest-1.0
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/conditionalaccesspolicy-update?view=graph-rest-1.0
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/device-update?view=graph-rest-1.0
//   https://github.com/Azure/Azure-Sentinel/blob/master/Solutions/Microsoft%20Entra%20ID/Analytic%20Rules/AdminPromoAfterRoleMgmtAppPermissionGrant.yaml
//   https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1528/
//   https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/001/
//
let LookbackWindow = 1h;
let ConsentActivities = dynamic(["Consent to application", "Add delegated permission grant", "Add app role assignment to service principal"]);
let CredentialActivities = dynamic(["Add service principal credentials", "Update application - Certificates and secrets management"]);
// The permissions in the table in 03 section 6: an application permission grant makes the application a Tier 0 workload identity (03 section 4)
let Tier0PermissionPattern = @"(?i)\b(RoleManagement\.ReadWrite\.Directory|AppRoleAssignment\.ReadWrite\.All|Application\.ReadWrite\.All|Directory\.ReadWrite\.All|UserAuthMethod-TAP\.ReadWrite\.All|UserAuthenticationMethod\.ReadWrite\.All|UserAuthMethod-TAP\.Read\.All|UserAuthenticationMethod\.Read\.All|Policy\.ReadWrite\.ConditionalAccess|Device\.ReadWrite\.All)\b";
// Permissions that reach ePHI in mail and files
let SensitivePermissionPattern = @"(?i)\b(Mail\.Read|Mail\.ReadWrite|Mail\.Send|Files\.Read\.All|Files\.ReadWrite\.All|Sites\.Read\.All|Sites\.ReadWrite\.All)\b";
// Application and service principal object IDs of every Tier 0 workload identity (placeholders; never empty)
let Tier0WorkloadObjectIdPattern = @"(?i)(tier0-application-object-id-placeholder|tier0-service-principal-object-id-placeholder)";
// Lower-case initiator names of sanctioned app-management automation (placeholder)
let ApprovedAppAutomation = dynamic(["app-lifecycle-automation-placeholder"]);
AuditLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(LookbackWindow)
| where OperationName in~ (ConsentActivities) or OperationName in~ (CredentialActivities)
| extend TargetText = tostring(TargetResources),
         TargetName = tostring(TargetResources[0].displayName),
         Actor = tolower(coalesce(tostring(InitiatedBy.user.userPrincipalName), tostring(InitiatedBy.app.displayName))),
         ActorIP = tostring(InitiatedBy.user.ipAddress)
| extend Activity = iff(OperationName in~ (ConsentActivities), "Consent or permission grant", "Credential added to app or service principal"),
         Tier0Permissions = extract_all(Tier0PermissionPattern, TargetText),
         SensitivePermissions = extract_all(SensitivePermissionPattern, TargetText),
         OnTier0Workload = TargetText matches regex Tier0WorkloadObjectIdPattern
| extend SuggestedSeverity = case(
    Activity == "Consent or permission grant" and array_length(Tier0Permissions) > 0 and OperationName =~ "Add app role assignment to service principal",
        "High: application permission grant that includes a Tier 0 permission; such a grant makes the application a Tier 0 workload identity (03 section 6)",
    Activity == "Consent or permission grant" and array_length(Tier0Permissions) > 0,
        "High: consent or delegated grant that names a Tier 0 permission; confirm the consenting administrator and the request",
    Activity == "Consent or permission grant" and array_length(SensitivePermissions) > 0, "High: grant includes mail or file permissions",
    Activity == "Credential added to app or service principal" and OnTier0Workload, "High: credential added to a Tier 0 workload identity",
    Activity == "Credential added to app or service principal" and Actor !in (ApprovedAppAutomation), "High: credential added outside approved automation",
    Activity == "Consent or permission grant", "Medium: consent without sensitive permissions, confirm the request",
    "Low: credential change by approved automation")
| where SuggestedSeverity !startswith "Low"
| project TimeGenerated, Activity, SuggestedSeverity, OperationName, Result, TargetName, Tier0Permissions, SensitivePermissions, OnTier0Workload,
    Actor, ActorIP, TargetResources, AdditionalDetails, CorrelationId
